# No leftover `__pycache__`

Running or importing Python creates `__pycache__/` and `*.pyc`. Git already ignores them. The agent still removes them before finishing the turn.

## When to clean

After any command that can compile Python: `python`, `python -m`, `pytest`, `pip install -e .`, imports, or scripts. Also after writing `.py` files if a run produced bytecode.

Clean before the final reply, including when the task failed partway through.

## How to clean

From the workspace root, delete every `__pycache__` directory and stray bytecode. On Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Force -Directory -Filter __pycache__ | Remove-Item -Recurse -Force
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Force -Include *.pyc,*.pyo -File | Remove-Item -Force
```

Skip virtualenvs (`.venv`, `venv`, `sqs_env`) and do not delete those trees.

## Do not

- Commit `__pycache__`, `*.pyc`, or `*.pyo`
- Leave a cache directory because a later step might reuse it
- Add `__pycache__` exceptions to `.gitignore` beyond the existing `__pycache__/` and `*.py[cod]` lines
